# Cell from playpick: mode=battleground map=flags spec=rogue:assassination
# style=mash view=844x390,touch save=carried.
#
# Checked direction.md's #7 and sessions.jsonl first: rogue:assassination has
# seven readings on record (melee/auto/idle/wander/flee, mode=raid/walk only)
# and none of them is a battleground -- this is the first rogue cell of any
# style on either battleground map. It also lands on the exact gap #7's
# 2026-09-28 entry closed for `melee` style but never asked of `mash`: that
# entry showed a melee-capable kit changes nothing under `melee` specifically
# (paladin:protection, hits=0 both pulls, same shape as the two ranged-spec
# priors). `mash` is a different steering shape, not just a different press
# pattern -- scripts/playbot.ts's want-computation (lines 928-998) has no
# branch for `mash` at all, so it releases the stick at the spawn point and
# round-robins ability slots 1-5 forever from wherever that is, same as the
# 2026-10-01-8 (druid:feral, conquest) and 2026-10-01-13 (warlock:destruction,
# this exact map) readings already on file. Both of those were ranged kits
# reading hits=0; this is the first time a melee weapon's own very short
# reach (scripts/playbot.ts:954, `far > 4` for `melee` style's own threshold)
# is paired with a style that never closes that gap on its own. If flags'
# spawn clusters bodies close enough (as 2026-10-01-12's druid:balance `good`
# pull found, dying at fightTime=16 right on top of the enemy), a melee body
# might land hits by accident where a ranged one could not; if it still reads
# zero, that is the sharper version of "melee style loses" #7 already asked
# for, now isolated to spawn proximity rather than reach at all.
